Approach Kaise Reliability Test impact analysis (TIA) — deterministic Coverage data se: kaunsa test kaunsi line touch karta hai. Changed lines → affected tests. 🟢 High — ye maths hai, AI nahi AI/ML prioritisation — heuristic Historical failure data, code churn, ownership, semantic similarity of diff to test names 🟡 Medium — probabilistic <p>Sahi answer: TIA base layer hai, AI usko rank karta hai. AI ko TIA replace karne mat do.</p> <pre><code># tools/prioritise_tests.py
"""
Layer 1 (deterministic): coverage map se affected tests nikalo
Layer 2 (AI/heuristic)  : unhe risk ke hisaab se rank karo
Layer 3 (safety)        : critical tests HAMESHA chalte hain, chahe kuch bhi ho
"""
import json
import subprocess
from pathlib import Path

# ---------------------------------------------- LAYER 3: always-run safety net
ALWAYS_RUN = [
    "tests/security/",          # permissions, tenant isolation
    "tests/smoke/",             # login, core navigation
    "tests/test_payments",      # money
]


def changed_files(base: str = "origin/main") -&gt; list[str]:
    out = subprocess.run(["git", "diff", "--name-only", f"{base}...HEAD"],
                         capture_output=True, text=True).stdout
    return [l for l in out.splitlines() if l.strip()]


# ------------------------------------------ LAYER 1: deterministic impact map
def affected_tests(changed: list[str], coverage_map: dict) -&gt; set[str]:
    """
    coverage_map: {"src/budget/service.py": ["tests/test_budget.py::test_a", ...]}
    Ye `coverage.py` ke contexts feature se generate hota hai — AI nahi.
    """
    hit = set()
    for f in changed:
        hit.update(coverage_map.get(f, []))
    return hit


# --------------------------------------------------- LAYER 2: AI ranking
RANK_PROMPT = """You are prioritising which tests to run first for a code change.

&lt;diff_summary&gt;
{diff}
&lt;/diff_summary&gt;

&lt;candidate_tests&gt;
{tests}
&lt;/candidate_tests&gt;

&lt;historical_signal&gt;
{history}
&lt;/historical_signal&gt;

Rank the tests most-likely-to-fail first. Consider:
- Which tests exercise the changed behaviour most directly
- Which tests have failed historically after similar changes
- Which tests cover the highest-risk areas (money, permissions, data integrity)

Return JSON only:
{{"ranked": [{{"test": "&lt;id&gt;", "risk": "high|medium|low", "why": "&lt;one sentence&gt;"}}]}}
Do NOT drop any test from the list. Ranking only — never exclusion.
"""
</code></pre>

# Optimisation Kya deta hai Kya leta hai 1 Binary search fallback (hybrid, upar wala code) Worst case O(n) → O(log n), best case wahi Ek counter 2 Interpolation ko sirf pehle step mein use karo, phir binary Ek achha jump, phir safe Simpler par kam fayda 3 Range clamp karo — <code>pos = max(lo, min(hi, pos))</code> Formula ke bad estimates se bachao Ek <code>min</code>/<code>max</code> 4 Quadratic / higher-order interpolation Non-linear distributions par behtar Bahut complicated, marginal fayda 5 Distribution transform — pehle values ko uniform banao (jaise log lo) Exponential data par kaam karta hai Transform pata hona chahiye 6 Interpolated binary search (dono ka average) Zyada robust Fayda kam 7 Bas binary search use karo Predictable, universal, simple Best case chhoot jata hai <p>Ek important practical variant — three-point interpolation:</p> <pre><code>def interpolation_search_clamped(arr, target):
    """pos ko range mein clamp karta hai — bad estimates se safety."""
    lo, hi = 0, len(arr) - 1
    while lo &lt;= hi and arr[lo] &lt;= target &lt;= arr[hi]:
        if arr[lo] == arr[hi]:
            return lo if arr[lo] == target else -1
        pos = lo + ((target - arr[lo]) * (hi - lo)
                    // (arr[hi] - arr[lo]))
        pos = max(lo, min(hi, pos))                 # 1
        if arr[pos] == target:
            return pos
        elif arr[pos] &lt; target:
            lo = pos + 1
        else:
            hi = pos - 1
    return -1
</code></pre> <ol> <li><code>pos = max(lo, min(hi, pos))</code> — clamp. Theoretically line 2 ki range check isse zaroori    nahi banati, par floating-point ya overflow issues ke case mein ye ek safety net hai.    Defensive programming — ek line, aur poora crash-class of bugs khatam.</li> </ol> <p>Interview answer: Interpolation search replaces binary search's midpoint with a position estimated from the values themselves. It assumes the data is uniformly distributed and computes where the target should be by linear interpolation between the endpoints: pos = lo + (target - arr[lo]) * (hi - lo) / (arr[hi] - arr[lo]). It's what a human does with a phone book — you don't open to the middle to look up "Aggarwal".</p> <p>On uniformly distributed numeric data it's O(log log n), because each step reduces the search space from n to roughly sqrt(n) rather than n/2. That's about five steps for a billion elements versus thirty for binary search — practically a constant.</p> <p>The catch is that it's fragile. On exponentially distributed data or data with a single large outlier, the estimate lands one position off each time and it degrades to O(n), which is worse than linear search because there's a division per step. It also requires numeric keys, since it does arithmetic on values, so it can't handle strings or arbitrary comparators the way binary search can.</p> <p>In production I'd normally just use binary search — predictable beats occasionally faster. If I did use interpolation, I'd cap the number of interpolation steps at about log n and fall back to binary midpoints after that, which preserves the best case and bounds the worst case at O(log n). That's the same defensive pattern as introsort.</p> <p>Cross-question: "Where does the O(log log n) come from?"</p> <p>From the fact that on uniform data the interpolation estimate's error is on the order of the square root of the current range, so the search space goes n, sqrt(n), n^(1/4), n^(1/8) and so on. To get down to a constant you need n^(1/2^k) to be about 2, which gives 2^k equal to log n, so k is log log n. It's a probabilistic result about the distribution of the estimate error, not a worst-case bound — which is exactly why the worst case is still O(n).</p>

Function Kaam <code>COALESCE(a, b, c)</code> pehla non-NULL return karta hai <code>NULLIF(a, b)</code> agar a = b toh NULL, warna a (divide-by-zero se bachne ke liye) <code>x IS DISTINCT FROM y</code> NULL-safe <code>&lt;&gt;</code> <code>x IS NOT DISTINCT FROM y</code> NULL-safe <code>=</code> <pre><code>SELECT supplier_id, name, COALESCE(rating, 0) AS rating_safe
FROM   suppliers;
</code></pre> supplier_id name rating_safe 1 Sharma Cement Co 4 2 Verma Steels 5 3 Nakoda Traders 0 4 Bombay Hardware 3 5 Gupta Sanitary 0 6 Ajmer Aggregates 2 <p>[REAL] Merlin mein documents ke optional fields — jaise ek purchase order ka <code>supplier</code> ya <code>deliveryDate</code> — MongoDB mein aksar field hi missing hoti hai, <code>null</code> bhi nahi. Isliye Mongo mein <code>{supplier: {$ne: "X"}}</code> aur <code>{supplier: {$exists: false}}</code> do alag cheezein hain. SQL ka NULL-trap Mongo mein "missing field vs null field" trap ban jaata hai — same bug, alag shakal. Interview mein ye connection banana bahut strong lagta hai.</p> <p>Interview answer: "NULL means unknown, not zero and not empty string. Any comparison with NULL evaluates to UNKNOWN, and WHERE only passes rows that are TRUE — so a filter like <code>status &lt;&gt; 'DRAFT'</code> silently drops every row where status is NULL. That's a real class of bug: the count on the dashboard doesn't match the count in the list. The fix is either an explicit <code>OR col IS NULL</code>, or <code>IS DISTINCT FROM</code>, which is NULL-safe. Aggregates also skip NULLs, so AVG divides by the count of non-null values, not the row count — I've seen that produce an average that looks impossibly high."</p> <p>Cross-question: "<code>COUNT(*)</code> aur <code>COUNT(column)</code> mein farak?" <code>COUNT(*)</code> saari rows ginta hai. <code>COUNT(column)</code> sirf wo rows ginta hai jahan wo column NULL nahi hai. Upar ke example mein 6 vs 4.</p> <p>Cross-question: "Do NULLs <code>GROUP BY</code> mein same group mein aayenge?" Haan. <code>=</code> ke liye NULL ≠ NULL, lekin <code>GROUP BY</code>, <code>DISTINCT</code> aur <code>UNION</code> NULLs ko ek dusre ke barabar treat karte hain. Ye SQL ki inconsistency hai jo interviewers ko poochna pasand hai.</p>

Surrogate Natural Kya hai meaningless auto-generated id (<code>SERIAL</code>, <code>UUID</code>, Mongo <code>ObjectId</code>) asli business value (email, PAN, invoice_no) Badalti hai? kabhi nahi badal sakti hai (email change, invoice renumber) Size chhota int, fast joins aksar bada string Privacy PII leak nahi PK URLs/logs mein PII daal deta hai Business meaning zero — extra unique constraint chahiye khud hi rule enforce karta hai <p>Industry practice (aur sahi jawab): surrogate PK use karo, aur natural key par ek alag UNIQUE constraint lagao. Dono ke fayde mil jaate hain — stable joins + enforced business rule.</p> <pre><code>CREATE TABLE invoices (
    invoice_id  BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,          -- surrogate
    invoice_no  VARCHAR(30) NOT NULL UNIQUE,    -- natural, still enforced
    …
);
</code></pre> <p>UUID vs auto-increment integer:</p> Auto-increment UUID / ObjectId Generate kahan DB (round-trip chahiye) client-side bhi ho sakta hai Index locality sequential — B-tree ke liye acha random — page splits, index bloat (UUIDv4) Enumeration risk <code>/orders/5</code> → <code>/orders/6</code> guess kar sakte ho (IDOR testing!) guess nahi kar sakte Distributed / sharding collision problem safe Size 4–8 bytes 16 bytes <p>QA note: auto-increment IDs ka matlab hai ki IDOR (Insecure Direct Object Reference) test karna zaroori hai — Org A ke user se Org B ke order ka id hit karo, 403/404 aana chahiye, 200 nahi. Mongo <code>ObjectId</code> bhi fully random nahi hai (timestamp + machine + counter), toh usko bhi security control mat maano — authorization check hi security hai.</p> <p>Interview answer: "A primary key uniquely identifies a row — one per table, implicitly unique and not null. A foreign key points at another table's key and gives you referential integrity, so the database refuses to create orphans. A composite key spans multiple columns, and the column order matters because it defines the index prefix. A unique key is like a primary key but you can have several per table and it permits NULLs — and since NULL isn't equal to NULL, most engines allow multiple NULL rows, which surprises people. On surrogate versus natural: I default to a surrogate primary key — a generated ID with no business meaning — because natural keys change, and a changing key breaks every reference to it. But I put a UNIQUE constraint on the natural key as well, so the business rule is still enforced by the database. The failure mode I've actually seen is code assuming uniqueness that the schema never guaranteed — a lookup returning Optional where there was no unique index behind it. When duplicate data appeared it threw an incorrect-result-size exception and surfaced as a 400 to the user. So now, whenever I see a single-result lookup, I go check that there's a matching unique index."</p> <p>Cross-question: "FK hamesha honi chahiye? Log kyun hata dete hain?" Log hataate hain: (a) write performance — har insert par parent check; (b) sharding — cross-shard FK possible hi nahi; (c) bulk load/migration ki asaani; (d) microservices — data alag DBs mein hai. Lekin QA ke liye ye red flag hai: FK hataoge toh orphan rows banenge, aur phir wo consistency application code mein enforce karni padegi — jo eventually koi na koi path miss kar deta hai. Agar FK nahi hai toh orphan-detection query nightly chalao (Section 37).</p>

Level Dirty read Non-repeatable read Phantom read Lost update Cost READ UNCOMMITTED ✅ possible ✅ possible ✅ possible ✅ sabse fast READ COMMITTED ❌ prevented ✅ possible ✅ possible ✅ fast (default: PG, Oracle, SQL Server) REPEATABLE READ ❌ ❌ prevented ✅ possible* ❌ (PG mein error) medium (default: MySQL) SERIALIZABLE ❌ ❌ ❌ prevented ❌ slowest, retries chahiye <p>* Postgres ka REPEATABLE READ (snapshot isolation) phantoms bhi rok deta hai. MySQL InnoDB gap locks se rokta hai. Standard allow karta hai — isliye "possible" likha hai.</p> <pre><code>-- Set karna
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
BEGIN;
…
COMMIT;

-- Ya session ke liye
SET SESSION CHARACTERISTICS AS TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;

-- Current level dekhna
SHOW transaction_isolation;
</code></pre>

Technique Kaise Consistent lock order hamesha ascending PK order mein rows chhoo. <code>ORDER BY order_id</code> laga kar batch update karo. Ye sabse bada fix hai. Transactions chhote rakho lock ka window jitna chhota, collision utna kam Transaction mein network call mat karo HTTP/email/payment gateway ke liye locks 2 second held rakhna = deadlock factory Sahi isolation level zaroorat se zyada strict level = zyada locks <code>SELECT FOR UPDATE</code> upfront jo rows chahiye, sabko ek hi statement mein, sorted order mein lock kar lo <code>NOWAIT</code> / <code>SKIP LOCKED</code> wait hi mat karo — turant fail ya skip Retry logic deadlock ek transient error hai. Exponential backoff ke saath 3 retries. Rows par kam contention hot counter row (<code>SELECT balance FROM totals WHERE id=1 FOR UPDATE</code>) ko sharded counters mein todo <p>Consistent ordering ka code example:</p> <pre><code>-- ❌ Deadlock-prone: order random hai
UPDATE orders SET status='APPROVED' WHERE order_id IN (2, 1);

-- ✅ Deterministic order
BEGIN;
SELECT order_id FROM orders WHERE order_id IN (1,2) ORDER BY order_id FOR UPDATE;
UPDATE orders SET status='APPROVED' WHERE order_id IN (1,2);
COMMIT;
</code></pre> <p>Application mein bhi: agar do accounts ke beech transfer hai, hamesha chhote account_id ko pehle lock karo — chahe paisa kis direction mein ja raha ho.</p> <pre><code>a, b = sorted([from_account, to_account])   # ← ek line jo deadlock khatam kar deti hai
lock(a); lock(b)
</code></pre>

Use Explanation Complexity chhupana 5-table join ek naam ke peeche Security user ko base table par nahi, view par access do — sensitive columns hata do Multi-tenancy / soft delete <code>WHERE is_deleted=false</code> ko ek jagah enforce karo, har query mein repeat mat karo Backward compatibility column rename kiya? purane naam se view bana do, purana code chalta rahe Consistent business logic "active order" ki definition ek jagah <p>Security view example:</p> <pre><code>CREATE VIEW v_users_public AS
SELECT user_id, name, role FROM users;    -- salary aur email nahi

REVOKE ALL ON users FROM reporting_role;
GRANT SELECT ON v_users_public TO reporting_role;
</code></pre>

Embed karo jab Reference karo jab Child hamesha parent ke saath padha jaata hai Child independently query hota hai Child ka parent ke bina wajood nahi (line items) Child kai parents se share hota hai (supplier) Array bounded hai (10s, 100s — hazaaron nahi) Array unbounded grow karta hai (audit log) Ek saath update hote hain (atomicity free mil jaati hai) Alag-alag update hote hain Document 16 MB limit ke andar rahega Document limit cross kar sakta hai <p>16 MB document limit — ye Mongo ki hard limit hai, aur ye design constraint hai. "Har order ke andar uske saare audit events embed kar do" ek din 16 MB pe crash karega. Ye QA ka test hai: unbounded array wale fields par volume test karo.</p> <p>Anti-pattern jo QA ko pakadna chahiye: the massive array. <pre><code>// ❌ Har din grow hota rahega, kabhi rukega nahi
{ _id: ..., projectName: "Skyline", auditLog: [ /* 40,000 entries */ ] }
</code></pre> Test: 10,000 events push karke dekho — document size, update latency, aur index behaviour.</p>

Level Kab QA kya dekhe ERROR Kuch fail hua, action chahiye Sabse pehle ye WARN Kuch galat hai par chal raha hai Aksar bug ka early sign INFO Normal flow milestones Trace follow karne ke liye DEBUG Detailed Debugging ke waqt on karo TRACE Bahut detailed Rarely <p>Structured logging — modern apps JSON logs likhte hain: <pre><code>{"timestamp":"2026-08-23T10:15:00Z","level":"ERROR","logger":"PaymentService",
 "message":"Charge failed","orderId":"PO-001","correlationId":"abc-123","userId":"u-45"}
</code></pre> Isse aap filter kar sakte ho — <code>correlationId=abc-123</code> daalke ek request ka poora journey.</p>

Achha fit Bura fit JSON/dict tree traverse (API response validation) Bade linear data (list processing) DOM tree walk Kuch bhi jahan depth 1000 se zyada ho sakti hai Nested config merge Performance-critical hot loops Directory tree scan (<code>pathlib.rglob</code> ka logic) Jab iterative version equally readable ho Divide and conquer (binary search, quicksort) Tail recursion (Python optimize nahi karta) <p>Practical QA example — nested API response validate karna:</p> <pre><code>def find_nulls(data, path=""):
    """Nested JSON mein saare null values ke paths dhoondho."""
    nulls = []
    if data is None:
        nulls.append(path or "&lt;root&gt;")
    elif isinstance(data, dict):
        for key, value in data.items():
            nulls.extend(find_nulls(value, f"{path}.{key}" if path else key))
    elif isinstance(data, list):
        for i, item in enumerate(data):
            nulls.extend(find_nulls(item, f"{path}[{i}]"))
    return nulls

response = {
    "user": {"id": 1, "email": None},
    "orders": [{"id": 10, "total": 99.5}, {"id": 11, "total": None}],
    "meta": None,
}
print(find_nulls(response))
# ['user.email', 'orders[1].total', 'meta']
</code></pre> <p>Line-by-line walkthrough:</p> <ol> <li><code>def find_nulls(data, path=""):</code> — <code>path</code> ek accumulator hai jo recursion ke saath    build hota hai. Default <code>""</code> immutable hai, safe.</li> <li><code>if data is None:</code> — base case. <code>is None</code> (1.5).</li> <li><code>nulls.append(path or "&lt;root&gt;")</code> — top-level <code>None</code> ke liye path khaali hoga, toh    <code>"&lt;root&gt;"</code> likha. <code>or</code> yahan safe hai kyunki khaali string ka matlab genuinely "no path" hai.</li> <li><code>elif isinstance(data, dict):</code> — dict par keys iterate karo. <code>isinstance</code> use kiya    <code>type(data) == dict</code> nahi, taaki <code>OrderedDict</code>/<code>defaultdict</code> bhi handle hon.</li> <li><code>f"{path}.{key}" if path else key</code> — root level par leading dot nahi chahiye. Ye chhoti si    detail output ko usable banati hai.</li> <li><code>nulls.extend(...)</code> — recursive call ki list ko flat mein jod diya. <code>append</code> likhte toh    nested lists milti.</li> <li><code>f"{path}[{i}]"</code> — list indices JSONPath-style. Ye bug report mein directly paste ho sakta    hai.</li> <li>Yeh function aapke API testing mein seedha kaam ka hai: response mein unexpected nulls    pakadna, ek assertion mein.</li> </ol> <p>Depth limit ke saath safe version:</p> <pre><code>def find_nulls_safe(data, path="", depth=0, max_depth=50):
    if depth &gt; max_depth:
        raise ValueError(f"nesting deeper than {max_depth} at {path!r}")
    ...
</code></pre> <p>Ye production code mein zaruri hai — malformed ya circular data se <code>RecursionError</code> ki jagah ek meaningful error milega.</p>
